#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124331 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124522 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "289ae874-f555-50ee-896f-eb2188004124" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 3x3 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/id #uuid "4b2fcf5d-0da3-5676-b4c6-54095c3fb45c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5486" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of the matrix \n![](images/9c/9c2a25dabbe750ebb52b4de6dbee954df55c09a8ceaf0b82eef346b5a337c544.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/94/947389b453c22e1b56704ec6a1cdf8e7e529ea9a944c57d3e55a471ab3539569.png)\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-9-\\lambda  & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda )(6-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +9)(\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=-9,$ $\\lambda_2=9,$ and $\\lambda_3=6.$ \n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124332 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124523 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5abea3ab-76a7-5598-8ede-b4cf88dd47d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues of a General 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "76c52a74-9b98-5df5-ac63-64e92af1a580" :question/math-academy-id "e-5487" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues ![](images/dc/dc652e29fc17e565962d96a947980358f776d5e37816269dbe9139328889fc3f.png) of the matrix \n![](images/a9/a93a99c9a1491b842d5b8d30c90f37b06528d0bd1b82bb5257d84ecb43a2b288.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix ![](images/0d/0d5fcfb7528ad4e3fcf0e7f173d9bd0be80d5148b6c1bda8a9bb765dc772d1e2.png)\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/61/619fe52f77e282e97915de285e8384d784dc6352eb06295e6991a1bdb2bae1c3.png)\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by ![](images/02/02f21e39c78b5ad8b88c5040d074fe78a5e6950760f2653b724c4e27627752ba.png) \n\nExpanding the determinant across the ![](images/ac/ac192f9c9318b0668e9dee7e695d4dd383f2d74df0298ef6dd4861d421e97afb.png)st row, we get the following:\n\n![](images/70/7062cb3cf1cf9e2dc2cac6c69537dd6c20c5b211f641a511092d7fbf8457d9de.png)\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of ![](images/97/97622f2aa4af27b2ce48c21c48e49129b58197dd2d02a3dddb5ff3685ba8197e.png) \n![](images/a4/a40f8317b6de6727323c39a9e8105822544f88727eb855ce5e89c71cc185c485.png)\n\nLet's pick ![](images/4b/4b7d6cca59677b4ec63fc9997d5d32c4d2e67f87d45b89540e42293f4ed1ba73.png) Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n![](images/14/146529d47426ad676072aeba20837a6da7ea5dc400a17f37dd3cb4d0a5c7f107.png)\nSo, ![](images/f1/f161fa211fd6dda217ad08855ed96703b8f620aeb1485d67562f3bf1666928d7.png) is a root of the equation. This means we can factor ![](images/21/210f39779d00613e2fcc094145f31e789ae93a0361618ae6b4623c107272799f.png) out of ![](images/b8/b8b832cc07ffad66665f2b961a9d78d97a0a89caac7ffc765e065a6d0946d0f6.png) using synthetic division:\n\n| ![](images/12/124a84b4788cb014c01f416a1e0433eb7991fd3c4c792400892377f4f3407221.png) | ![](images/e1/e12ac0c95825bef841bc95fa94b20bf787e03ec405c223a26fab4a73c61efa10.png) | ![](images/78/783e116511a2ce8a0ad54bedf9e79e7b8d380fc31dae50f43d1c4ce51a305354.png) | ![](images/cc/cc8c6a49685acb757524d62a8cbb6805c5198485f888874bbbb66968aa9e6178.png) | ![](images/ae/aeffa7b19c9d0bea2c544ec3cc1aa53ad8b44271b89f115e7a4db58ecfd33720.png) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | ![](images/c8/c84a10b5fd2ed823a1f173f8120dd2250031ad7e5f10424ef0d3cd91ec148bed.png) | ![](images/2f/2f84eb7bb5c17949d98cacb1f217bbbd99ef39032a3a0a960412d0e94bcba8b6.png) | ![](images/df/df9b41873d2d60b97b90650e0713641a8647a38205427f8f8fe04cef9237646b.png) | ![](images/ee/ee595b41249fe71b2b1dbc2d31b9eb9069bd31e887f4bf62456ab05dd926318e.png) |\n|  | ![](images/1b/1baad26e6ade455fffeb4304606352ecfbf86e67e4cb47c35b736f59e44d009b.png) | ![](images/af/af11b6c6dc78667e7d9c0d4acfeccea8ce8e6f83f49e3213083c8bff30c4fd14.png) | ![](images/30/30accb457d578da8be84827af0d559e9f57f0d18d08f8df490dc88879f8c90e7.png) | ![](images/27/278088cea7543d913bd6e9702b03c58ff40e05413a543120087373c426bad83e.png) |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n![](images/b7/b7457336851f79803231c1d2d64a07591ff7bb5e1f3315dd8af98bfe2782bcb4.png)\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ![](images/65/6528f51e5d19fcda74b8ca490c4fd89b3e43269b57053da77b29284702c1f833.png) ![](images/8c/8c25f2182438cde814f01fd81ad6e94684ceed686db04002e7934b310e3261b1.png) and ![](images/5e/5e20e499636e1ce842b9d25a9934ce6956334c31f56d9cfd22f5230fbcbce983.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124380 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/id #uuid "a1c51187-c0a6-505c-bc58-2319b439086e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7047" :question/problem "![](images/23/2379f9f0cec3f257da5a4c0695f114d0bc7f3b4c914edabfd690ff94705faceb.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda=4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- $\\lambda=-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- The eigenvalue $\\lambda=6$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, P-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/8e/8ee9ef991440e5641d6a458c0384382b54eb3d8504856b942fc33cf24b0b85e9.png)\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(P-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4 \\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3 \\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-\\lambda )\\cdot (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 7 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-35) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda=3,$ $\\lambda=4,$ $\\lambda=6,$ and $\\lambda=-6$ (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124523 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5abea3ab-76a7-5598-8ede-b4cf88dd47d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues of a General 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94ce24cb-8a23-5a2f-ac9f-9ceca2dac150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ^{3} - 5λ^{2} - 2λ = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122679} {:answer/id #uuid "d285350f-2004-5dfc-a4e1-a6c5e711d77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5λ^{2} - 2λ - 4 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122680} {:answer/id #uuid "1d7a1922-362c-5527-b2d1-b7711e35a36f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ^{3} - 2λ - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122681} {:answer/id #uuid "437ce86d-4e1a-572a-9208-40adc1749e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ^{3} - 5λ^{2} + 2λ - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122682} {:answer/id #uuid "65b10513-37f5-5057-9555-edecf0f1c511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ^{3} + 2λ^{2} - λ - 2 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122683}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b55f74a2-418f-5fce-b67f-29118bc0950f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122678}] :question/id #uuid "f39262c6-06ce-51e6-92a2-5f2acfb813a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36824" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124522 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "289ae874-f555-50ee-896f-eb2188004124" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 3x3 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16474b71-dd87-5c02-90a1-1986cdcc2b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321329} {:answer/id #uuid "455b312d-436f-5a59-beed-3c43b768e4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +1)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321696} {:answer/id #uuid "9521fe4a-7fce-5da7-a5cc-1b5de6565706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +2)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322336} {:answer/id #uuid "9e18c66c-1798-5646-b90c-5aa3dcdc792b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322392} {:answer/id #uuid "eb00f264-ac0e-52cf-bbb6-b0d74f11988e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -2)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e18c66c-1798-5646-b90c-5aa3dcdc792b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322392} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b0b363-ceb3-501c-b8ad-22246e96a7de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a91b44ac-1def-566a-a0ac-b4f797a0bab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-36860" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $2$nd column, we obtain the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(-2)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fca3927-3f6f-5b44-b4b9-d2ba4ecdca76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366008} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b39b02-0c43-5154-abf8-74818045d6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366009} {:answer/id #uuid "5de737fa-c3ea-5d82-bb67-a96903882581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366010} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a00a64-39c3-55cd-9978-fe5453d5a40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366011} {:answer/id #uuid "08349bc9-dea7-5693-842f-2e153b69045e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6b39b02-0c43-5154-abf8-74818045d6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366009} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfcd45ad-3392-56d7-9f7a-f558cdf77f96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e41ff16d-c666-5918-91e3-4e187ca2af95" :question/math-academy-id "q-37387" :question/problem "$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $C$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C$\n- $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C$\n- The eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,C-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nC-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 6 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(C-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $4$th column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 6 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-\\lambda )\\cdot (5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )^{2}\\cdot ((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+20) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +14) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0$ for the polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +14$ we have that the only real eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (with algebraic multiplicity $2$). \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124523 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5abea3ab-76a7-5598-8ede-b4cf88dd47d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues of a General 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02fadbdc-d540-572d-a6fc-e90b0bea45aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}-53\\lambda -36=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321174} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8b6827-a8f5-5e9e-a456-3c1f4b0bd806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}-37\\lambda -108=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321371} {:answer/id #uuid "53cbf2a7-4b85-5a35-aa42-5f47e5014747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}-37\\lambda +72=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321806} {:answer/id #uuid "5844cdec-8e7d-5d66-a293-f8b40e411e1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}-37\\lambda -72=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321840} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8c4ccf-02d4-5b71-8706-613880e25eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+53\\lambda +36=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5844cdec-8e7d-5d66-a293-f8b40e411e1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}-37\\lambda -72=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321840} :answer-field/id #uuid "11bd07c2-d426-5569-a039-db8a048a82ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04cda9cf-e19f-5987-86a4-1bd75a963670" :question/math-academy-id "q-37396" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-9-\\lambda  & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(B-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-9-\\lambda  & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-9|\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +8)-9(-16) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+37\\lambda -72)+144 & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+37\\lambda +72 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}-37\\lambda -72 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27af9af5-40f7-5590-87de-4650f27e5aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366367} {:answer/id #uuid "a879185a-10d3-51bd-a415-70db009b2238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366368} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7d7228-7d99-5e51-bc6b-b09cd75c534e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366369} {:answer/id #uuid "152a4a84-a477-515b-8d42-be94175385d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366370} {:answer/id #uuid "c815e277-06a0-5dd2-ba04-f1aa13bc716f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "152a4a84-a477-515b-8d42-be94175385d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366370} :answer-field/id #uuid "abafa309-3832-58cf-8a1e-a1f4d098470e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6753a04-a08e-5bc2-a11a-2cad03fb8d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80800" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- $\\lambda =-14$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-\\lambda )\\cdot (-14-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-14-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +14)\\lambda (\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =2,$ $\\lambda =-2,$ $\\lambda =0,$ and $\\lambda =-14$  (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
